## Function convention: definition-only authority

N20 prose supplies cube identities but no function definition. Adjacent modern
N18/N19 have geometric-progression prose, not a function definition.
Their lesson SHA256 values are respectively
`27c99d900c9b8e1662a3b5ae35fdaf0fa720691ef82718415505e2da447e6298`
and `66f877329a232d15b9688a920826af18b559270b703418fb6a88115e34ba5932`.

Under the expressly authorized read-only terminology lookup, the retained
`ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/6a/lessons/alg6-c2-s1-n8/lesson.json`
(SHA256 `5292f911a80c2210dbac35010417e9a673fa5a7e48e0615275e988faebe73c3a`),
printed page 45, supplies:

- Prose index 5: "If a relation between the elements of two sets makes each
  element of the first set correspond to at most one element of the second
  set, that relation is called a function." (Translation of retained definition.)
- Index 7 clarifies: each element of the function's actual domain corresponds
  to exactly one element of its range.
- Index 10: a function with domain X and range Y is also a mapping of set X onto Y.

Thus the definition is set-to-set single-valuedness, not a restriction to
real-number inputs and real-number outputs. In 320(a), inputs are POINTS P,
not their x coordinates; in 320(b), inputs are real NUMBERS x and outputs
are POINTS. The two directions must not be interchanged with the numerical
relation x -> y graphed by ABC.

Lookup limitation disclosure: the first local prose locator was overly broad
and emitted additional non-definition lesson exposition (including examples
and derivation paragraphs), though it read no exercise files, answer files,
choices, or old PASS. None of that exposition was used as solving authority.
The exact three general-definition paragraphs above were then isolated.
No re-extraction, page rendering, or page approval occurred.

## Judging contract considered before candidate read

Read-only production contract:
`app/src/lib/textbook-answer.ts`, SHA256
`bee990b6367f8111ab30a4409c28d5e9387a01068c0bf27376326dfe4fe8ff8f`;
`app/src/lib/answer-normalize.ts`, SHA256
`5c11b4304874b65ec5d99f92413b524cfc4f6683a60268a0ba04143534c7448c`.

`expression` uses parsed mathematical equivalence via `isEqual`, without
checking polynomial standard form, factorization, one-fraction form, working
method, proof, drawing, or a separately supplied original domain.
`exact` checks normalized lexical forms; it can enforce declared concrete
forms but is not a general method/proof checker. Real MathLive finalize
remains parent-owned and has not been claimed here. `ungraded` still needs
a complete display answer. For method/proof/graph requirements, avoid an
auto verdict based only on an equivalent final scalar or expression.
For form-only questions, exact candidates may be suitable after the parent's
native finalize; otherwise use ungraded rather than fake form enforcement.

## 309 (2 method tasks)

For every real x:

a) Distribute, then collect like terms:
$(x-10)(x^2+10x+100)
=x^3+10x^2+100x-10x^2-100x-1000=x^3-1000$.

b) Use the difference-of-cubes identity with a=x, b=10:
$(x-10)(x^2+10x+100)=x^3-10^3=x^3-1000$.

Both requested methods must appear; two identical scalar/result keys do not
verify the two methods. Safe current grading: ungraded with both derivations.

## 310 (6 standard-polynomial tasks)

For each item set u,v to the bases in the cubic identity. Mixed terms cancel,
so $(u-v)(u^2+uv+v^2)=u^3-v^3$ and
$(u+v)(u^2-uv+v^2)=u^3+v^3$ identically for all real variables.

a) u=x,v=2: $x^3-8$.
b) u=p,v=5: $p^3-125$.
c) u=y,v=4, sum: $y^3+64$.
d) u=2a,v=1, sum: $8a^3+1$.
e) u=10x,v=3y, difference: $1000x^3-27y^3$.
f) u=4a,v=5b, sum: $64a^3+125b^3$.

Answers must be standard-form polynomials, not the unchanged source products.
Expression equivalence alone cannot enforce that requirement.

## 311 (12 cube-factorization tasks)

The following are actual products obtained by the indicated cube bases;
expanding each product returns u^3 +/- v^3, so these are full symbolic
identities, not numeric samples. All variables are arbitrary real numbers.

a) (u,v)=(x,y), difference:
$(x-y)(x^2+xy+y^2)$.
b) (c,2), difference:
$(c-2)(c^2+2c+4)$.
c) (x,3), sum:
$(x+3)(x^2-3x+9)$.
d) (2x,3), difference:
$(2x-3)(4x^2+6x+9)$.
e) (5a,1), sum:
$(5a+1)(25a^2-5a+1)$.
f) (n,4m), difference:
$(n-4m)(n^2+4mn+16m^2)$.
g) (y/2,10), difference:
$\left(\frac y2-10\right)\left(\frac{y^2}{4}+5y+100\right)$.
h) (x,1/3), sum:
$\left(x+\frac13\right)\left(x^2-\frac x3+\frac19\right)$.
i) (c,0.1), difference, since $0.1^3=0.001$:
$(c-0.1)(c^2+0.1c+0.01)$.
j) (0.2b,5), sum, since $0.2^3=0.008$:
$(0.2b+5)(0.04b^2-b+25)$.
k) (0.4,ny), difference, since $0.4^3=0.064$:
$(0.4-ny)(0.16+0.4ny+n^2y^2)$.
l) (4x,y/3), sum:
$\left(4x+\frac y3\right)\left(16x^2-\frac43xy+\frac{y^2}{9}\right)$.

Plain expression equivalence is insufficient to require factorization.
These concrete products can support exact form candidates, subject to native
finalize, or a complete ungraded standard answer.

## 312 (4 divisibility proofs)

a) Sum of cubes:
$327^3+173^3=(327+173)(327^2-327\cdot173+173^2)
=500\cdot80287$. Integer second factor proves divisibility by 500.

b) Difference of cubes:
$731^3-611^3=(731-611)(731^2+731\cdot611+611^2)
=120\cdot1354323$. This proves divisibility by 120.

c) Difference of cubes:
$4759^3-2779^3=(4759-2779)(4759^2+4759\cdot2779+2779^2)
=1980\cdot43596183$. This proves divisibility by 1980.

d) A first sum-of-cubes factor is only 600, not 1200. Compute the
second factor or show it is even:
$386^3+214^3=600(386^2-386\cdot214+214^2)
=600\cdot112188=1200\cdot56094$.
Both bases are even, hence their quadratic factor is divisible by 4,
which also supplies the required extra factor of 2. Ungraded proof.

## 313 (2 rational-method calculations)

a) Because $59-41=18$,
the radicand is
$59^2+59\cdot41+41^2+59\cdot41
=(59+41)^2=10000$.
Its principal square root is 100.

b) Because $67+52=119$,
the radicand is
$67^2-67\cdot52+52^2-67\cdot52
=(67-52)^2=225$.
Its principal square root is 15.

Both radicands are nonnegative and the displayed roots are positive.
The prompt requests the most rational method, so the cube identity and
square recognition must appear; numeric-only judging verifies a result,
not the method requested.

## 314 (4 cancellations, original REAL domains)

a) Factor $y^3-1000=(y-10)(y^2+10y+100)$.
Result $y^2+10y+100$, preserving $y\ne10$.

b) Factor $8x^3+27=(2x+3)(4x^2-6x+9)$ and
$6x+9=3(2x+3)$.
Result $\frac{4x^2-6x+9}{3}$, preserving $x\ne-\frac32$.

c) $a^3-216=(a-6)(a^2+6a+36)$.
The quadratic is $(a+3)^2+27>0$ on R.
Result $\frac1{a-6}$, domain $a\ne6$.

d) Numerator $2(9b^2+12b+16)$;
denominator $(3b-4)(9b^2+12b+16)$.
The quadratic is $(3b+2)^2+12>0$.
Result $\frac2{3b-4}$, domain $b\ne\frac43$.

The excluded points in a,b remain excluded although their displayed
simplifications extend there. Equivalence alone cannot check preservation
of the original domain or actual reduction of the input fraction.

## 315 (4 one-fraction tasks, original REAL domains)

a) Common denominator $(p-q)(p^2+pq+q^2)$ gives numerator
$p^2+pq+q^2-3pq=(p-q)^2$.
Result $\frac{p-q}{p^2+pq+q^2}$, with $p\ne q$.
On R, $p^2+pq+q^2=(p+q/2)^2+3q^2/4$ vanishes only at
(0,0), already excluded by $p\ne q$. The new fraction would otherwise
allow p=q other than zero, but the original does not.

b) $a^3+8=(a+2)(a^2-2a+4)$.
Common numerator:
$6a^3+12a-3a^2(a+2)=3a^3-6a^2+12a
=3a(a^2-2a+4)$.
Result $\frac{3a}{a+2}$; $a\ne-2$.
The canceled quadratic $(a-1)^2+3$ is always positive on R.

c) $x^3-1=(x-1)(x^2+x+1)$.
Common numerator:
$2x^2+7x+3-(1-2x)(x-1)-3(x^2+x+1)
=2x^2+7x+3+2x^2-3x+1-3x^2-3x-3=x^2+x+1$.
Result $\frac1{x-1}$; $x\ne1$.
The canceled quadratic $(x+1/2)^2+3/4$ is always positive.

d) $y^3-8=(y-2)(y^2+2y+4)$.
Common numerator:
$(y-2)^2-6y+(y^2+2y+4)
=2y^2-8y+8=2(y-2)^2$.
Result $\frac{2(y-2)}{y^2+2y+4}$, with $y\ne2$.
The quadratic $(y+1)^2+3$ is positive. Cancellation does not restore y=2.

All four are single fractions. An unchanged difference/sum of fractions,
even if mathematically equivalent, has not done the requested conversion.

## 316 (6 product tasks)

a) Apply sum of cubes to $u=x^2,v=y^2$:
$x^6+y^6=(x^2+y^2)(x^4-x^2y^2+y^4)$.

b) Difference of squares in the cubes, then cube factorizations:
$a^6-b^6=(a^3-b^3)(a^3+b^3)
=(a-b)(a+b)(a^2+ab+b^2)(a^2-ab+b^2)$.

c) As b with a=c,b=1:
$c^6-1=(c-1)(c+1)(c^2+c+1)(c^2-c+1)$.

d) Sum of cubes with u=n^2,v=1:
$n^6+1=(n^2+1)(n^4-n^2+1)$.

e) Difference of squares of $y^3$ and 8, then cubes:
$y^6-64=(y^3-8)(y^3+8)
=(y-2)(y+2)(y^2+2y+4)(y^2-2y+4)$.

f) $(2k)^6-n^6$ as b:
$(2k-n)(2k+n)(4k^2+2kn+n^2)(4k^2-2kn+n^2)$.

All are identities on R. The instruction only says to write a product;
it does not demand irreducible factorization over R (e.g. introducing
sqrt(3) in a,d). Equivalent unexpanded original powers are not products.

## 317 (4 binomial-times-trinomial tasks)

a) Set u=a+6,v=1 in the cube difference:
$(a+5)((a+6)^2+(a+6)+1)=(a+5)(a^2+13a+43)$.

b) Set u=y-2,v=3 in the cube sum:
$(y+1)((y-2)^2-3(y-2)+9)=(y+1)(y^2-7y+19)$.

c) Set u=x+3,v=4 in the cube difference:
$(x-1)((x+3)^2+4(x+3)+16)=(x-1)(x^2+10x+37)$.

d) Set u=x,v=x-y in the cube sum:
$(2x-y)(x^2-x(x-y)+(x-y)^2)=(2x-y)(x^2-xy+y^2)$.

The simplified final forms are concrete products of a binomial and a
trinomial, valid for arbitrary real variables. Pure equivalence does not
enforce this specific shape.

## 318 (3 identity-method tasks)

Identity (1) follows for all real a,b by telescoping multiplication:
every intermediate $a^{n-j}b^j$ cancels, leaving $a^n-b^n$.
No nonzero or unequal restrictions survive in the polynomial identity.

a) a=x,b=1,n=6: $x^6-1$.
b) a=y,b=2,n=4: $y^4-16$.
c) a=a,b=3,n=5: $a^5-243$.

The requested use of identity (1) should be stated, not auto-judged solely
from an equivalent final expression.

## 319 (2 cancellations)

a) $x^5-1=(x-1)(x^4+x^3+x^2+x+1)$ by telescoping.
Result $x^4+x^3+x^2+x+1$, domain $x\ne1$.

b) $y^7-1=(y-1)(y^6+y^5+y^4+y^3+y^2+y+1)$,
and $2y-2=2(y-1)$.
Result $\frac{y^6+y^5+y^4+y^3+y^2+y+1}{2}$,
domain $y\ne1$.

The polynomial extensions at 1 are not the original fractions' domain.

## 320 (2 directed-correspondence tasks)

a) YES under the actual retained set-to-set definition.
Let L=BA union BC. Each point P in L has one well-defined x coordinate,
so $P\mapsto x(P)$ is a function $L\to[1,6]$.
It is not injective: distinct points may have the same coordinate.
This is not a numerical function R -> R, nor a claim that ABC is the
graph of a numerical function y=f(x).

b) NO for the relation as stated; the description does not choose a unique
branch. Parameterizing the segments:
BA: $y=2+\frac32(x-1)=\frac{3x+1}{2}$, $1\le x\le3$.
BC: $y=2-\frac15(x-1)=\frac{11-x}{5}$, $1\le x\le6$.
Their y difference is $\frac{17(x-1)}{10}$.
Thus for every $1<x\le3$ the same input x has two DISTINCT output points.
For example x=2 corresponds to $(2,\frac72)$ on BA and
$(2,\frac95)$ on BC. At x=1 both segments meet at the one point B;
for $3<x\le6$ only BC supplies a point. All x in [1,6] occur, but
existence is not uniqueness. An explicit branch-selection rule could
define a different function; the stated relation supplies none.

Graph-dependent explanation is safest ungraded; do not reverse the
point-to-coordinate direction in a.

## 321 (graph construction + interval image: 2 tasks)

$y=x^2-2x-15=(x-1)^2-16$, with full real domain.
Draw the upward parabola with vertex (1,-16), symmetry axis x=1,
x intercepts (-3,0),(5,0), and y intercept (0,-15).
Plot symmetric pairs such as (0,-15),(2,-15), (-1,-12),(3,-12)
and connect smoothly; this specifies the actual drawing, not merely a
generic recipe to "plot points".

For x in [-1,6], x-1 lies in [-2,5], so
$0\le(x-1)^2\le25$. Minimum -16 is attained at x=1;
maximum 9 is attained at x=6. The other endpoint x=-1 gives -12,
not the minimum. Every y in [-16,9] is achieved by
$x=1+\sqrt{y+16}\in[1,6]\subset[-1,6]$.
Therefore the image of [-1,6] is the CLOSED interval [-16,9].
Ungraded because the exercise also requires an actual graph.

## 322 (2 rationality proofs)

a) Expand conjugate squares:
$(5+2\sqrt6)^2+(5-2\sqrt6)^2
=(25+20\sqrt6+24)+(25-20\sqrt6+24)=98$.
Since 98 is an integer, it is rational.

b) The two inner radicands are positive: $7>2\sqrt{10}$
because $49>40$. Let U=7+2sqrt(10), V=7-2sqrt(10).
Using principal nonnegative square roots,
$(\sqrt U+\sqrt V)^2=U+V+2\sqrt{UV}
=14+2\sqrt{49-40}=14+6=20$.
Alternatively $U=(\sqrt5+\sqrt2)^2$ and
$V=(\sqrt5-\sqrt2)^2$ with sqrt(5)>sqrt(2), so the sum
of the principal roots is 2sqrt(5). Thus its square is 20.
Both values are rational, and both proofs must be shown. Ungraded.

